  <section>
    <sectiontitle>Aflictions</sectiontitle>
    <indexentry><entry>Afflictions</entry></indexentry>

    <p>
      Diseases, poisons, and acids 
      phobias, madness?
      IS AN ASPECT AN AFLICTION?
      
      Transmission.
      - Injury, Contact, Ingested, and Inhaled.

      Airborne
      : Tiny droplet nuclei or dust stay in the air for a long time and travel far on air currents. Examples include measles and tuberculosis.Fomites (surfaces): Germs live on non-living objects like doorknobs, phones, or toys until touched by a new host. Examples include norovirus and the common cold.Vehicles: Contaminated food, water, or blood products carry pathogens to multiple people. Examples include cholera and hepatitis A.
      Vectors: Living creatures—mostly insects like mosquitoes, ticks, and fleas—carry pathogens between hosts. Examples include malaria and Lyme disease.

    </p>
    

    <subsection>
      <subsectiontitle>Acids</subsectiontitle>
      <indexentry><entry>Acids</entry></indexentry>

      <p>
	There are four strong acids in common use: Oil of Vitriol, Aqua
	Fortis, Spirit of Salt, and Aqua Regis. The first three must be stored
	in special glass bottles.  While Aqua Regia must be used immediately
	once it is made as it constantly gives off gas and the pressure of
	these gases will destroy any container that is capable of holding it.
      </p>
      
      <p>
	Acids may be either thrown, or poured into a solution or over an item
	(such as a chain, or a door lock).  
      </p>

      <p>
	<indexentry><entry>Acids</entry><subentry>Pouring</subentry></indexentry>
	When pouring acid a single crafting check is made when opening the
	vial.  If the acid has been created by a PC using the Alchemy ability
	then the check is made using that characters Alchemy Craft check.  If
	the acid has been made by an NPC then the player makes a check for the
	NPC creator at Rank 0.  A fail represents poor quality acid that is
	weak the GM can determine the effect of the reduced strength acid.
	Critical fails have different effects described for each type of acid.
      </p>

      <p>	  
	<indexentry><entry>Acids</entry><subentry>Throwing</subentry></indexentry>
	When throwing acid a two checks are made. The first is a Throw Object
	check to see where the object lands, the second is a crafting check
	with results dependent on the type of acid.
      </p>

      <p>
	<indexentry><entry>Acids</entry><subentry>Broken Vial</subentry></indexentry>  
	If some mishap causes a vial of acid to break on the characters person
	then 1d4 random colocated items are also destroyed and possibly the
	PC suffers acid damage as well.
      </p>

